s on which line(s)? | Verification |\n|-----------------|------------------------|--------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) | \\(x=1\\) true; \\(y=1\\) true; \\(y=x\\) gives \\(1=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(y=x\\) gives \\(2=2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines, and exactly one of the lines (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny. Hence the required configuration exists for \\(n=3\\) with \\(k=1\\). \u220e\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have reviewed the Summary and Detailed Solution sections to ensure they contain no gaps, all statements are rigorously justified, and the required three-section format (including this Self-Correction Instruction) is strictly followed. The construction and verification are complete and correct."
