((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) are collinear horizontally, which would make the line non‑sunny.  \n  Thus the required covering is impossible.\n\n* **Subcase A3:** \\(c=3\\) is symmetric to Subcase A1 and leads to the same contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B – \\(L\\) is vertical, \\(L:x=c\\).\n\nThe possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(1,2,3\\).\n\n* **Subcase B1:** \\(c=1\\).  \n  Then \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  As in Subcase A1, any pair among these three points lies on a forbidden line, so the two sunny lines could each cover at most one point – contradiction.\n\n* **Subcase B2:** \\(c=2\\).  \n  Then \\(L\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The four points left are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  Again each sunny line can contain at most two points, so both must contain exactly two.  \n  The only sunny pair among the four points is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\) – **not** sunny).  \n  All other possible pairs are vertical or horizontal, i.e. non‑sunny.  \n  Hence a covering with two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n* **Subcase B3:** \\(c=3\\) is symmetric to Subcase B1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case C – \\(L\\) is diagonal, \\(L:x+y=d\\).\n\nThe possible values of \\(d\\) are \\(2,3,4\\).\n\n* **Subcase C1:** \\(d=2\\).  \n  Then \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The five remaining points must be covered by two sunny lines, but by Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most two points, giving a total capacity of \\(4<5\\).  Contradiction.\n\n* **Subcase C2:** \\(d=3\\).  \n  Then \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The four points left are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  To use the two sunny lines efficiently each must contain two points.  \n  The only sunny pair among the four points is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The remaining two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \n  Hence the required covering cannot be achieved.\n\n* **Subcase C3:** \\(d=4\\).  \n  Then \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The three points left are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  As in Subcase A1, any pair among these three points lies on a forbidden line, so the two sunny lines could each cover at most one point – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll possible positions for the unique non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction.  \nTherefore no set of three distinct lines can cover the six points with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
