;\\le\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2t}\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      n-1, & n\\ \\text{even}\\;(t=n/2),\\\\[2mm]\n      n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}\\;(t=(n-1)/2).\n     \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\) and for odd \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Existence of the extreme values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   All of them are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); none is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the two axis\u2011parallel lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   They cover all points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \n   The remaining points have \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and satisfy \\(a+b\\ge4\\).  \n   For the sum \\(a+b=4\\) the only uncovered point is \\((2,2)\\); cover it with the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   The other sums \\(5,6,\\dots ,n+1\\) are covered by the diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of them sunny.\n\n   *Maximum number of sunny lines.*  \n\n   \u2013 **\\(n\\) odd.**  \n     Use the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) given by  \n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=x+2,\\dots ,y=x+(n-1).\n     \\]\n\n     For a fixed offset \\(d\\) the line \\(y=x+d\\) contains the integer points\n     \\((a,a+d)\\) with \\(a\\ge1,\\;a+d\\ge1\\) and \\(2a+d\\le n+1\\); this yields exactly\n     \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points for each line, and the collection of the\n     \\(n\\) different offsets covers every lattice point of the triangle.\n     All slopes are \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   \u2013 **\\(n\\) even.**  \n     Proceed as for the odd case, but omit the line \\(y=x+\\frac{n}{2}\\) (its slope would be\n     \\(1\\) but it would contain only one point).  \n     Replace the omitted line by the axis\u2011parallel line \\(x=1\\) (or \\(y=1\\)).  \n     The resulting family consists of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) and one non\u2011sunny\n     v