                "content": ".\n\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{array}{c}\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\text{ are all possible};\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\text{ are all possible ( }k=n\\text{ is impossible).}\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Why these are the only possibilities  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\n*For a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let*  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|S_n|=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (the points form a right‑angled triangular lattice).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nHence a non‑sunny line is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A capacity bound for sunny lines  \n\nFor a sunny line \\(L\\) write its equation as \\(y=mx+c\\) with\n\\(m\\neq0,\\;m\\neq\\infty,\\;m\\neq-1\\).\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\cap L\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); eliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+ma\\le n+1-c\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m}.\n\\]\n\nThus the number of integer points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on a sunny\nline is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(The bound is attained by the line \\(y=x\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, and by\n\\(y=x\\) when \\(n\\) is even, giving \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A necessary inequality for the existence of a configuration  \n\nAssume we have a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) already contains\n\\(n\\) points).  \nUsing (1) we obtain the **capacity inequality**\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If (2) fails, the required configuration cannot exist.*\n\nEvaluating (2) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nn\\text{ odd} & \\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{n+1}{2}\n&\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-k)n\n\\\\[2mm]\\hline\nn\\text{ even}&\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{n}{2}\n&\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\frac{n}{2}+(n-k)n .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth inequalities are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n\\qquad\\text{(always true)}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nk\\le n-1\\ \\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n* for **odd** \\(n\\) the inequality imposes **no restriction** on \\(k\\);\n* for **even** \\(n\\) it forces \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nThus the only candidates are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\[1mm]\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n-1 & (n\\text{ even}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions showing that every value in (4) is attainable  \n\nWe now give explicit families of lines that realise each admissible\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  The “horizontal backbone’’  \n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate\n\\(b\\le n-t\\).  \nThe points that remain are exactly  \n\n\\[\nR_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b>n-t\\}\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t,\\; n-t+1\\le b\\le n,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple count shows  \n\n\\[\n|R_{t}|=\\frac{t(t+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Covering the remainder with sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (n-t+ i)-i = x + (n-2t+i).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**\n(because slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nThe integer points of \\(R_{t}\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,\\,n-t+1),\\;(i+1,\\,n-t+2),\\dots ,\\;(i+t-i,\\,n-t+t-i)\\}\n      =\\{(i,\\,n-t+1),\\dots ,(t,\\,n-i+1)\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. exactly the points of \\(R_{t}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).\nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\) covers **all** points of\n\\(R_{t}\\), and each line contains at most \\(\\lceil t/2\\rceil\\) points,\nwhich is certainly not larger than the bound (1).\n\nThus the set of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-t}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\\;\n\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{L_1,\\dots ,L_t}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(t\\) are\nsunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3  Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nChoose \\(t=k\\) for any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) in the construction\n(8).  The resulting configuration has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nHence **all** values \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are attainable.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nThe same construction works for every \\(t=k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\nWhen \\(k=n\\) we would need \\(n\\) sunny lines; each sunny line can hold\nat most \\(n/2\\) points by (1), so the total capacity would be at most\n\\(n\\cdot n/2 = n^{2}/2\\), which is **strictly smaller** than the\nrequired \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot\nexist.  Therefore the attainable values are exactly\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Summary of the result  \n\n* For **odd** \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be\nrealised.\n* For **even** \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be\nrealised, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThese are precisely the values described in the boxed answer above.  "
