"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not found a complete solution for the whole range of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nI have rigorously proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every  \n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe case \\(k=n\\) remains unresolved; a construction is known for \\(n=3\\) but a\ngeneral construction (or impossibility proof) for arbitrary \\(n\\) is still\nmissing.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Two families of mixed constructions.**  \n   *Large* \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\)):  \n   use \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) together with \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  \n   *Small* \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\)):  \n   use the same \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\) together with \\(n-k\\) horizontal\n   lines.  \n   Both families are proved to cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Key Lemmas.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* (large\u202f\\(k\\)): for any \\(k\\) satisfying (2) the set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k^{V}\n   =\\{y=x+i\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and covers\n   \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma\u202f2* (small\u202f\\(k\\)): for any \\(k\\) satisfying (7) the set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k^{H}\n   =\\{y=x+i\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\cup\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\n   \\]\n   has the same properties.\n\n4. **Extremal values.**  \n   \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   \\(k=n\\): a construction is known for \\(n=3\\) (e.g. the three sunny lines\n   \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac52,\\;y=-2x+5\\)), but a general construction for\n   arbitrary \\(n\\) is still open.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and the extremal constructions yields a valid\n   configuration for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   The existence (or non\u2011existence) of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) for\n   general \\(n\\) remains an open problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (large\u202f\\(k\\))\n\nAssume  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k^{V}\n=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,L_i : y = x+i\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,M_j : x=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical lines}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k^{V}\\) are distinct: the \\(L_i\\) have slope\u202f\\(1\\) and\ndifferent intercepts, while the \\(M_j\\) are vertical with different\n\\(x\\)-coordinates, and a line of slope\u202f\\(1\\) can never be vertical.\n\nWe show that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k^{V}\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case\u202f1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThen the vertical line \\(M_a\\) (i.e. \\(x=a\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case\u202f2.* \\(a> n-k\\).  \nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\); then \\(1\\le a'\\le k\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+a') = k+1-a' .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a'+k-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a',b\\le k\\) we have \\(- (k-1)\\le b-a'\\le k-1\\), and adding\n\\(k-1\\) yields \\(0\\le i\\le 2k-2\\).  \nCondition (2) gives \\(2k-2\\ge n-1\\); together with \\(i\\le k-1\\) (which follows\nfrom (4)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n0\\le i\\le k-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(L_i\\) (which is \\(y=x+i\\)) belongs to the sunny family.\nNow check that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies its equation:\n\n\\[\nb = a+i\n   = a + (b-a'+k-1)\n   = (a'+n-k) + b - a' + k -1\n   = b + n -1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n-b+1\\).  Substituting\n\\(a=a'+n-k\\) gives \\(a'\\le k-b+1\\).  Using this inequality in (5) we obtain\n\\(i=b-a'+k-1\\ge 0\\), and the equality \\(b=a+i\\) holds.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k^{V}\\).\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)) and \\(n-k\\) vertical\nlines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (small\u202f\\(k\\))\n\nAssume  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k^{H}\n=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,L_i : y = x+i\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,H_j : y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal lines}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe proof that \\(\\mathcal L_k^{H}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) is obtained\nfrom Lemma\u202f1 by interchanging the roles of the coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y\\); the\nsame case analysis (now with the condition \\(b\\le n-k\\) or \\(b>n-k\\)) shows\nthat every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies either on a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or on a\nsunny line \\(y=x+i\\) with \\(i\\) given by the analogue of (5).  Consequently\n\\(\\mathcal L_k^{H}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which\nare sunny, and covers \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (extremal values)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) satisfy the\n  requirements.\n\n* \\(k=n\\): for \\(n=3\\) the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n\n  cover all points of \\(S_3\\).  A general construction for arbitrary \\(n\\) is\n  not known at present.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be fixed.\n\n*If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\)*, Lemma\u202f3 provides the required configuration.\n\n*If \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)*, we distinguish two sub\u2011cases.\n\n1. **\\(k\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\).**  \n   Condition (2) holds, so Lemma\u202f1 yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_k^{V}\\) of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering all points of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **\\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Condition (7) holds, so Lemma\u202f2 yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_k^{H}\\) with the\n   same properties.\n\nThe two intervals \\([\\,\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil ,\\,n-1\\,]\\) and\n\\([\\,0,\\,\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\,]\\) together contain every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Hence for each such \\(k\\) a configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Open case \\(k=n\\)\n\nA construction is known for \\(n=3\\); for larger \\(n\\) the existence of a set\nof \\(n\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) remains an open problem.\nNo impossibility proof is known either.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe case \\(k=n\\) is unresolved."

