ts. A sunny line could be y = 2x - 1? Let's see: points (1,1) satisfies y=2*1-1=1, okay. (2,3) would be (2,3) but (2,3) not in S_3 because a+b=5 >4. So not. Let's try line y = x + 1: points (1,2),(2,3) but (2,3) not in S_3. So only one point. Maybe line y = -2x + 5: points (1,3) (2,1) both in S_3? (1,3) satisfies -2*1+5=3, yes. (2,1) satisfies -2*2+5=1, yes. So line y = -2x + 5 (slope -2) passes through (1,3) and (2,1). That's two points. So maximum maybe 2. floor((n+1)/2) = floor(4/2)=2. So matches.\n\nFor n=4, S_4 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 10 points. Can we find a sunny line with 3 points? Let's try slope -2: y = -2x + c. For integer points, we need c integer. For (1,3): c = 5. Then line y = -2x +5 passes through (1,3) and (2,1). Also (3,-1) not in region. So only 2 points. Try slope -3: y = -3x + c. For (1,4): c=7 => line y = -3x+7 passes through (1,4) and (2,1). That's 2 points. Try slope 2: y = 2x + c. For (1,1): c=-1 => line y=2x-1 passes through (1,1) and (2,3) but (2,3) is in S_4? (2,3) has a+b=5 ≤5? Actually n+1=5, so (2,3) is allowed. So (2,3) is in S_4. Also (3,5) not allowed. So line has 2 points. Try slope 1/2: y = (1/2)x + c. For integer points, need x even. (2,2): c=1 => line y = x/2 + 1 passes through (2,2) and (4,3) but (4,3) not allowed because a+b=7 >5. So only 1 point. So max maybe 2. floor((n+1)/2) = floor(5/2)=2. So matches.\n\nFor n=5, floor((6)/2)=3. Can we find a sunny line with 3 points? Let's try slope -2: y = -2x + c. For (1,5): c=7 => line passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). All three are in S_5? (1,5) a+b=6 ≤6, yes. (2,3) a+b=5 ≤6, yes. (3,1) a+b=4 ≤6, yes. So line y = -2x + 7 passes through three points. So max at least 3. Could there be 4? Try slope -3: y = -3x + c. For (1,5): c=8 => line passes (1,5),(2,2) (a+b=4), (3,-1) not allowed. So only 2. Try slope -1/2: y = -0.5x + c. For integer points, need x even. (2,4): c=5 => line passes (2,4),(4,3) (a+b=7 >6) not allowed. So only 1. So max