unny. Pair (4,2) with (2,4): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny). Pair (4,2) with (3,3): slope (2-3)/(4-3) = -1/1 = -1 (non-sunny). Pair (4,2) with (1,4): slope (2-4)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666... which is not -1,0,∞, so sunny. So line L4 could be (1,4),(4,2). Let's see if that works.\n\nLet's propose the following lines:\n\n- Non-sunny: y=1 (horizontal)\n- Sunny L1: (1,5),(2,3) slope -2\n- Sunny L2: (1,4),(2,2) slope -2? Actually slope (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2, same slope but different line? Let's compute line through (1,4) and (2,2): slope -2, line equation y = -2x + 6 (since at x=1, y=4 => 4 = -2*1 + b => b=6). So L2: y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not in S_5.\n\n- Sunny L3: (1,3),(3,2) slope -0.5 (line y = -0.5x + 3.5)\n- Sunny L4: (1,2),(4,2) slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed. So need a different pair for L4. Let's try (1,2) with (3,3): slope (2-3)/(1-3) = -1/ -2 = 0.5 (sunny). So L4: line through (1,2) and (3,3) slope 0.5, equation y = 0.5x + 1.5 (since at x=1, y=2 => 2 = 0.5*1 + b => b=1.5). So L4 passes through (1,2),(3,3). Also passes through (5,4) not in S_5.\n\nNow check coverage:\n\n- y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1).\n- L1 covers (1,5),(2,3).\n- L2 covers (1,4),(2,2).\n- L3 covers (1,3),(3,2).\n- L4 covers (1,2),(3,3).\n\nNow list all points:\n\n(1,1) covered by y=1.\n(1,2) covered by L4.\n(1,3) covered by L3.\n(1,4) covered by L2.\n(1,5) covered by L1.\n(2,1) covered by y=1.\n(2,2) covered by L2.\n(2,3) covered by L1.\n(2,4) not covered yet. (2,4) is not in any line. We need to cover (2,4). Could we adjust L1 to cover (2,4) instead of (2,3)? Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover (2,4). Could pair (2,4) with (1,2): slope (4-2)/(2-1) = 2/1 = 2 (sunny). So we could replace L4 (1,2),(3,3) with L4': (1,2),(2,4). Then (3,3) becomes uncovered. But maybe we can cover (3,3) with L2? L2 currently covers (1,4),(2,2). Could we adjust L2 to cover (3,3) as well? Let's see line through (1,4) and (3,3): slope (4-3)/(1-3) = 1/ -2 = -0.5 (sunny). That lin