hrough (3,1): y = -3*3 + 10 = 1, yes. Also passes through (4,-2) not in S_5. So only two points.\n\nPotential triple: (2,3),(4,2) slope (3-2)/(2-4) = 1/ -2 = -0.5 (sunny). So line y = -0.5 x + c => at (2,3): 3 = -1 + c => c = 4. So line y = -0.5 x + 4 passes through (4,2): y = -0.5*4 + 4 = -2 + 4 = 2, yes. Also passes through (6,1) not in S_5. So only two points.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(3,1) slope (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(4,1) we already considered.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(3,1) not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(5,1) slope (2-1)/(2-5) = 1/ -3 = -1/3 (sunny). So line y = -1/3 x + c => at (2,2): 2 = -2/3 + c => c = 8/3. At x=5, y = -5/3 + 8/3 = 3/3 = 1 => (5,1) covered by L_h. So line L2: passes through (2,2),(5,1) and (1,? ) maybe (1,? )? Let's check x=1: y = -1/3 + 8/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So only two points.\n\nPotential triple: (2,3),(5,1) we considered earlier.\n\nPotential triple: (2,4),(5,1) slope (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/ -3 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (3,1),(5,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (3,3),(5,1) slope (3-1)/(3-5) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (4,1),(5,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nThus it seems difficult to find triples among the remaining points. Perhaps we need to use a different non-sunny line, not x=1. Let's try using diagonal line x+y = 6 (i.e., the line with slope -1, which is non-sunny). That line includes points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points. Then we need to cover the remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 10 points. Use 4 sunny lines to cover them. Each sunny line can have at most 3 points, total capacity 12 >10, so possible.\n\nNow we need to partition these 10 points into 4 sunny lines. Let's try to find triples.\n\nPoints left: Let's list them:\n\nA: (1,1)\nB: (1,2)\nC: (1,3)\nD: (1,4)\nE: (2,1)\nF: (2,2)\nG: (2,3)\nH: (3,1)\nI: (3,2)\nJ: (4,1)\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines.\n\nPotential triple: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) but (3,3) is on diagonal line (non-sunny) and already covered. So we can use line y = x (sunny) covering (1,1),(2,2). That's a pair.\n\nPotential triple: (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) not allowed (3,4 not in S_5). So only pair (1,2),(2,3) slope 1.\n\nPotential triple: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) slope (3-2)/(1-2) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (1,4),(2,2) slope (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line L1: y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not in S_5. So pair.\n\nPotential triple: (1,4),(3,2) slope (4-2)/(1-3) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (1,4),(4,1) slope (4-1)/(1-4) = 3/ -3 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) not allowed (4,3 not in S_5). So only pair (2,1),(3,2) slope 1.\n\nPotential triple: (2,1),(4,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,1),(3,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,1),(4,2) slope (1-2)/(2-4) = -1/ -2 = 0.5 (sunny). So line L2: y = 0.5 x passes through (2,1),(4,2). Already (4,2) is on diagonal line (non-sunny) and covered. So L2 covers (2,1) and (4,2) (but (4,2) already covered). So L2 covers (2,1) only new.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(3,1) slope (2-1)/(2-3) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPotential triple: (2,2),(4,1) slope (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/ -2 = -0.5 (sunny). So line L3: y = -0.5 x + c => at (2,2): 2 = -1 + c => c = 3. So line y = -0.5 x + 3 passes through (4,1): y = -0.5*4 + 3 = -2 + 3 = 1, yes. Also passes through (6,0) not in S_5. So L3 covers (2,2) and (4,1). (4,1) not covered yet. So L3 covers (2,2) and (4,1).\n\nPotential triple: (2,3),(3,1) slope (3-1)/(2-3) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line L4: y = -2x + c => at (2,3): 3 = -4 + c => c = 7. So line y = -2x + 7 passes through (3,1): y = -2*3 + 7 = 1, yes. Also passes through (4,-1) not in S_5. So L4 covers (2,3) and (3,1).\n\nNow we have used lines:\n\n- Diagonal line x+y=6 (non-sunny) covering (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\n- Sunny line L1: y = -2x + 6 covering (1,4),(2,2).\n- Sunny line L2: y = 0.5 x covering (2,1) (and (4,2) already covered).\n- Sunny line L3: y = -0.5 x + 3 covering (2,2) (already covered by L1) and (4,1).\n- Sunny line L4: y = -2x + 7 covering (2,3),(3,1).\n\nNow let's see which points are covered:\n\nFrom diagonal: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\n\nFrom L1: (1,4),(2,2). (1,4) new, (2,2) new.\n\nFrom L2: (2,1) new.\n\nFrom L3: (4,1) new (2,2 already covered).\n\nFrom L4: (2,3) new, (3,1) new.\n\nNow we have covered: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1),(1,4),(2,2),(2,1),(4,1),(2,3),(3,1). That's 11 points. Uncovered points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,2). Also (3,2) not covered. (1,1),(1,2),(1,3) not covered. That's 4 points left. We have used 4 sunny lines already (L1-L4). We have no more sunny lines left (k=4). So we need to cover these 4 points with the non-sunny line(s). But we only have one non-sunny line (the diagonal). It doesn't cover these points. So this configuration fails.\n\nThus we need a different approach.\n\nGiven the difficulty, we need to verify if k=4 is indeed possible for n=5. The answer set claims it is possible. Let's try to find a construction for n=5,k=4. Perhaps we can use two non-sunny lines and two sunny lines? But k=4 means exactly 4 sunny lines, so we have only 1 non-sunny line. So we need to cover all points with 4 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. The non-sunny line can be horizontal, vertical, or diagonal. Let's try to use a vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering (1,1)-(1,5). Then we need to cover the remaining 10 points with 4 sunny lines. That's similar to earlier attempt with x=1, but we couldn't find triples. Let's try again with a different set of sunny lines.\n\nRemaining points after x=1: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). We need to cover these with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to use line y = x (sunny) covering (2,2),(3,3). Also (4,4) not in S_5. So L_a: (2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\nWe can use line y = -x + 6 (slope -1) not allowed (non-sunny). So avoid.\n\nWe can use line y = 2x - 3 (slope 2) covering (2,1),(3,3) (but (3,3) already covered). Also (4,5) not in S_5. So L_b: (2,1),(3,3). But (3,3) already covered, but okay.\n\nWe can use line y = -2x + 8 (slope -2) covering (2,4),(3,2),(4,0). So L_c: (2,4),(3,2). Good.\n\nWe can use line y = -0.5 x + 3 (slope -0.5) covering (2,2),(4,1). But (2,2) already covered, (4,1) new. So L_d: (4,1) (and (2,2) already covered). So far we have used 4 sunny lines: L_a (2,2),(3,3), L_b (2,1),(3,3), L_c (2,4),(3,2), L_d (4,1). Let's see coverage:\n\n- x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,5).\n- L_a covers (2,2),(3,3).\n- L_b covers (2,1),(3,3) (3,3 already covered).\n- L_c covers (2,4),(3,2).\n- L_d covers (4,1).\n\nNow uncovered points: (2,3),(3,1),(4,2),(5,1). Also (2,3) not covered, (3,1) not covered, (4,2) not covered, (5,1) not covered. That's 4 points left, but we have used all 4 sunny lines. So fails.\n\nWe need to adjust lines to cover more points.\n\nMaybe we can use a sunny line that covers three points among the remaining set. For example, line y = -2x + 8 covers (2,4),(3,2). That's only two. Could we find a line covering (2,4),(4,2),(6,0) not in S_5. So only two.\n\nLine y = -0.5 x + 3 covers (2,2),(4,1). Only two.\n\nLine y = 0.5 x + 0.5? Let's see line through (2,1) and (4,2): slope (1-2)/(2-4) = -1/ -2 = 0.5. Equation y = 0.5 x + b => at x=2, y=1 => 1 = 1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 0.5 x passes through (2,1),(4,2). Also passes through (6,3) not in S_5. So only two.\n\nLine y = x - 1 passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) not in S_5. So only two.\n\nLine y = x + 1 passes through (2,3),(3,4) not in S_5. So only (2,3) maybe.\n\nLine y = -x + 5 passes through (2,3),(3,2),(4,1). That's slope -1 (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\nLine y = -x + 6 passes through (2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's slope -1 (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\nLine y = -x + 7 passes through (2,5) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = 2x - 3 passes through (2,1),(3,3),(4,5) not in S_5. So only (2,1),(3,3).\n\nLine y = 2x - 5 passes through (2,-1) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = -2x + 6 passes through (2,2),(3,0) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = -2x + 10 passes through (2,6) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = 3x - 5 passes through (2,1),(3,4) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = -3x + 10 passes through (2,4),(3,1),(4,-2). So L_e: (2,4),(3,1). That's two points.\n\nLine y = -3x + 13 passes through (2,7) not in S_5.\n\nLine y = 1/3 x + something? Let's try line through (3,1) and (5,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (5,1): slope (3-1)/(2-5) = 2/ -3 = -2/3 (sunny). Equation: y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 2) => y = -2/3 x + (4/3 + 3) = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=5, y = -10/3 + 13/3 = 3/3 = 1 => (5,1). So line L_f: passes through (2,3),(5,1) and also (??) maybe (3,? )? For x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So only two points.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (5,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,2) and (5,1): slope (2-1)/(4-5) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (4,1): slope (3-1)/(2-4) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (5,1) horizontal not allowed.\n\nLine through (3,2) and (5,1): slope (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/ -2 = -0.5 (sunny). So line L_g: passes through (3,2),(5,1). Also passes through (1,3) which is covered by x=1. So L_g covers (3,2) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have lines:\n\n- Non-sunny: x=1 covering (1,1)-(1,5).\n- Sunny L_a: y = x covering (2,2),(3,3).\n- Sunny L_b: y = -2x + 8 covering (2,4),(3,2).\n- Sunny L_c: y = 0.5 x covering (2,1),(4,2). Wait (4,2) is not in S_5? Actually (4,2) is in S_5 (4+2=6). So L_c covers (2,1),(4,2). Good.\n- Sunny L_d: line through (3,2),(5,1) slope -0.5, but (3,2) already covered by L_b. So maybe we can replace L_b with L_d? Let's see.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines. Let's try to pick them as:\n\n1. L1: y = x (covers (2,2),(3,3)).\n2. L2: y = -2x + 8 (covers (2,4),(3,2)).\n3. L3: y = 0.5 x (covers (2,1),(4,2)).\n4. L4: line through (5,1) and (2,3) slope -2/3 (covers (2,3),(5,1)). This is L_f.\n\nNow check coverage:\n\n- x=1 covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5).\n- L1 covers (2,2),(3,3).\n- L2 covers (2,4),(3,2).\n- L3 covers (2,1),(4,2).\n- L4 covers (2,3),(5,1).\n\nNow list all points:\n\n(1,1) covered.\n(1,2) covered.\n(1,3) covered.\n(1,4) covered.\n(1,5) covered.\n(2,1) covered by L3.\n(2,2) covered by L1.\n(2,3) covered by L4.\n(2,4) covered by L2.\n(3,1) not covered yet.\n(3,2) covered by L2.\n(3,3) covered by L1.\n(4,1) not covered.\n(4,2) covered by L3.\n(5,1) covered by L4.\n\nRemaining uncovered: (3,1) and (4,1). Both have y=1, which is horizontal line y=1, but that's a non-sunny line. However we already used the non-sunny line x=1, but we could have used y=1 instead. But we have only one non-sunny line allowed (k=4). So we need to cover (3,1) and (4,1) with sunny lines. Let's see if we can adjust.\n\nWe could replace the non-sunny line x=1 with y=1 (horizontal). Then we would cover (3,1) and (4,1) automatically. But then we lose coverage of (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Those need to be covered by sunny lines. Let's try using y=1 as the non-sunny line, and adjust sunny lines to cover the points in column x=1.\n\nNow non-sunny: y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). So points (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) remain uncovered. We need to cover them with sunny lines.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines to cover the remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) plus the other points (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2). That's total 10 points left. We need to cover them with 4 sunny lines, each up to 3 points, total capacity 12 >10. So possible.\n\nLet's try to find sunny lines covering column x=1 points.\n\nWe can use line y = -x + 6 (slope -1) not allowed. So we need to use other slopes.\n\nWe can pair (1,2) with (2,4) slope (2-4)/(1-2) = -2/ -1 = 2 (sunny). So line L1: y = 2x + b => at (1,2): 2 = 2*1 + b => b = 0. So line y = 2x passes through (1,2),(2,4). Also passes through (3,6) not in S_5. So L1 covers (1,2),(2,4).\n\nWe can pair (1,3) with (3,2) slope (3-2)/(1-3) = 1/ -2 = -0.5 (sunny). So line L2: y = -0.5 x + c => at (1,3): 3 = -0.5 + c => c = 3.5. So line y = -0.5 x + 3.5 passes through (3,2) (since -0.5*3 + 3.5 = -1.5 + 3.5 = 2). Also passes through (5,1) which is covered by y=1. So L2 covers (1,3),(3,2).\n\nWe can pair (1,4) with (2,2) slope (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line L3: y = -2x + c => at (1,4): 4 = -2 + c => c = 6. So line y = -2x + 6 passes through (2,2) (since -2*2 + 6 = 2). Also passes through (3,0) not in S_5. So L3 covers (1,4),(2,2).\n\nWe can pair (1,5) with (3,3) slope (5-3)/(1-3) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed. So we need a different pairing for (1,5). Maybe pair (1,5) with (2,3) slope (5-3)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line L4: y = -2x + c => at (1,5): 5 = -2 + c => c = 7. So line y = -2x + 7 passes through (2,3) (since -2*2 + 7 = 3). Also passes through (3,1) which is covered by y=1. So L4 covers (1,5),(2,3).\n\nNow we have used 4 sunny lines: L1 (1,2),(2,4); L2 (1,3),(3,2); L3 (1,4),(2,2); L4 (1,5),(2,3). Let's check coverage of all points:\n\n- Non-sunny y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1).\n- L1 covers (1,2),(2,4).\n- L2 covers (1,3),(3,2).\n- L3 covers (1,4),(2,2).\n- L4 covers (1,5),(2,3).\n\nNow list all points:\n\n(1,1) covered.\n(1,2) covered.\n(1,3) covered.\n(1,4) covered.\n(1,5) covered.\n(2,1) covered.\n(2,2) covered.\n(2,3) covered.\n(2,4) covered.\n(3,1) covered.\n(3,2) covered.\n(3,3) not covered! (3,3) is missing.\n(4,1) covered.\n(4,2) not covered.\n(5,1) covered.\n\nWe have uncovered (3,3) and (4,2). We have used all 4 sunny lines, so we cannot add more. However we could adjust some lines to include these points.\n\nWe need to incorporate (3,3) and (4,2) into the existing sunny lines, possibly by replacing some pairings.\n\nObserve that (3,3) can be paired with (1,5) on line slope (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed. Pair (3,3) with (2,1) slope (3-1)/(3-2) = 2/1 = 2 (sunny). So line y = 2x - 3 passes through (2,1) (but (2,1) is covered by y=1). Actually (2,1) is on y=1, which is non-sunny. But we can still use it as a point on a sunny line; overlapping is allowed. So we could replace one of the sunny lines with a line that includes (3,3) and (2,1) and maybe another point.\n\nLet's see: L1 currently is y = 2x covering (1,2),(2,4). Could we modify L1 to be y = 2x - 3, which passes through (2,1) and (3,3). Check: at x=2, y = 2*2 - 3 = 1 => (2,1). At x=3, y = 6 - 3 = 3 => (3,3). Also passes through (1,-1) not in S_5. So L1' would cover (2,1) (already covered) and (3,3). It would not cover (1,2) or (2,4). So we would lose coverage of (1,2) and (2,4). We need to cover those elsewhere.\n\nWe could try to cover (1,2) and (2,4) using another line. For example, line through (1,2) and (4,2) is horizontal, not allowed. (1,2) and (3,2) horizontal, not allowed. (1,2) and (5,1) slope (2-1)/(1-5) = 1/ -4 = -0.25 (sunny). So line L5: passes through (1,2),(5,1). Also passes through (2,? ) maybe (2,? )? Let's compute: line equation: y - 2 = -0.25 (x - 1) => y = -0.25 x + 2.25. At x=2, y = -0.5 + 2.25 = 1.75 not integer. So only (1,2),(5,1). (5,1) already covered by y=1. So we could use L5 to cover (1,2). Then we need to cover (2,4) elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, we could pair (2,4) with (4,2) slope (4-2)/(2-4) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nPair (2,4) with (3,2) slope (4-2)/(2-3) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line L6: y = -2x + c => at (2,4): 4 = -4 + c => c = 8. So line y = -2x + 8 passes through (3,2). So L6 covers (2,4),(3,2). But (3,2) is already covered by L2. Overlap allowed. So we could replace L2 (which currently covers (1,3),(3,2)) with L6 covering (2,4),(3,2). Then we need to cover (1,3) elsewhere.\n\nLet's try to systematically find a set of 4 sunny lines covering all points after using y=1 as non-sunny.\n\nGoal: cover points:\n\nColumn x=1: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5)\nRemaining points: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2).\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines to cover these 10 points.\n\nWe can try to use lines that each cover 3 points, maybe some cover 2.\n\nPotential triples:\n\n- Triple (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. So only pair.\n\n- Triple (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) slope? (1,3) to (2,2): slope (3-2)/(1-2) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). So not.\n\n- Triple (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed.\n\n- Triple (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) slope (5-3)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line y = -2x + c => at (1,5): 5 = -2 + c => c = 7. So line L_a: y = -2x + 7 passes through (2,3) and (3,1). (3,1) is covered by y=1. So L_a covers (1,5),(2,3). Good.\n\n- Triple (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed.\n\n- Triple (1,3),(2,5) not allowed.\n\n- Triple (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed.\n\n- Triple (2,2),(3,3),(4,4) not allowed (4,4 not in S_5). So only pair (2,2),(3,3).\n\n- Triple (2,3),(3,2),(4,1) slope? (2,3) to (3,2): slope (3-2)/(2-3) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). So not.\n\n- Triple (2,4),(3,2),(4,0) not allowed.\n\n- Triple (3,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed.\n\n- Triple (3,3),(4,2) slope (3-2)/(3-4) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\n- Triple (2,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed.\n\n- Triple (2,4),(4,2) slope (4-2)/(2-4) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nThus many pairs only.\n\nThus we need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each covering at most 2 points (since triples are rare). That would give at most 8 points covered, insufficient. So we need at least some triples. The only triple we found is (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) but (3,1) is covered by y=1. So that line covers only 2 new points. So we need to find a triple that includes three points not on y=1. Let's search for triples among the set of points we need to cover.\n\nSet of points to cover: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2). Let's search for triples collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can try to find a line with slope 2 that passes through (1,2) and (2,4) (we have that). Does it also pass through (3,6) not in S_5. So only two.\n\nSlope 0.5 line through (2,1) and (4,2) we used earlier, but (2,1) is covered by y=1, so only one new point (4,2). So not helpful.\n\nSlope -2 line through (1,5) and (2,3) and (3,1). (3,1) is covered, so only two new points.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (1,3) and (3,2) and (5,1). (5,1) covered, so only two new points.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (1,4) and (3,3) and (5,2) not in S_5. Actually (3,3) is in set, (1,4) is in set. So line y = -0.5 x + c: at (1,4): 4 = -0.5 + c => c = 4.5. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 4.5 = 3 => (3,3). So line L: y = -0.5 x + 4.5 passes through (1,4),(3,3). Also passes through (5,2) not in S_5. So only two new points.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (1,5) and (3,4) not in S_5.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (2,4) and (4,3) not in S_5.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (2,3) and (4,2) we have: y = -0.5 x + 4 => passes through (2,3),(4,2). That's two new points.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (2,2) and (4,1) (4,1 covered). So only one new.\n\nSlope -0.5 line through (3,2) and (5,1) we have: (3,2) new, (5,1) covered.\n\nSlope -2 line through (2,4) and (3,2) we have: (2,4) new, (3,2) new.\n\nSlope -2 line through (1,4) and (2,2) we have: (1,4) new, (2,2) new.\n\nSlope -2 line through (1,3) and (2,1) (2,1 covered). So only (1,3) new.\n\nSlope -2 line through (1,2) and (2,0) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 2 line through (1,2) and (2,4) we have: (1,2) new, (2,4) new.\n\nSlope 2 line through (1,3) and (2,5) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 2 line through (1,4) and (2,6) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 2 line through (1,5) and (2,7) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 1 line through (2,2) and (3,3) we have: (2,2) new, (3,3) new.\n\nSlope 1 line through (1,2) and (2,3) we have: (1,2) new, (2,3) new.\n\nSlope 1 line through (1,3) and (2,4) we have: (1,3) new, (2,4) new.\n\nSlope 1 line through (1,4) and (2,5) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 1 line through (1,5) and (2,6) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 1 line through (3,2) and (4,3) not in S_5.\n\nSlope 1 line through (4,2) and (5,3) not in S_5.\n\nThus we have many pairs of slope 1 lines covering pairs: (1,2)-(2,3), (1,3)-(2,4), (2,2)-(3,3). Also slope 2 line (1,2)-(2,4). Slope -2 lines (1,4)-(2,2), (2,4)-(3,2). Slope -0.5 lines (2,3)-(4,2), (1,4)-(3,3). Slope -0.5 line (1,3)-(3,2). Slope -0.5 line (1,5)-(3,4) not in S_5.\n\nThus we can try to cover the 10 points using 4 lines, each covering 3 points maybe by using overlapping points. Overlap allowed, so we can have lines that share points, which reduces the number of distinct points covered per line. But we need to cover all points, not necessarily each point covered exactly once. Overlap can help to reduce the number of lines needed, but we have a fixed number of lines (4). Overlap can cause some points to be covered multiple times, but we need to ensure all points are covered at least once.\n\nThus we can try to use lines that cover many points, even if they overlap. For example, line y = x + 1 covers (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) not in S_5, so only (1,2),(2,3). That's two points. But we can also use line y = x + 2 covering (1,3),(2,4). That's two points. And line y = x covering (2,2),(3,3). That's two points. That's three lines covering (1,2),(2,3),(1,3),(2,4),(2,2),(3,3). That's 6 points. Then we need to cover (1,4),(1,5),(3,2),(4,2). That's 4 points left, we have one line left (since we have 4 sunny lines total). Can we cover these 4 points with one sunny line? Probably not. So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can use a line that covers three of those four points. For example, line through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not in S_5, so only (1,4),(2,2). That's two points. Line through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) we have (1,5),(2,3) new, (3,1) covered by y=1. So that line covers (1,5),(2,3). But (2,3) already covered by line y = x + 1. Overlap okay.\n\nLine through (3,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed. So we need to cover (3,2) and (4,2) maybe with line through (1,4),(3,2),(5,0) not in S_5. So line y = -0.5 x + 4.5 covers (1,4),(3,3) not (3,2). Actually we need (3,2). Let's compute line through (1,5) and (4,2): slope (5-2)/(1-4) = 3/ -3 = -1 (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,2): slope (4-2)/(1-4) = 2/ -3 = -2/3 (sunny). Equation: y - 4 = -2/3 (x - 1) => y = -2/3 x + 4 + 2/3 = -2/3 x + 14/3. At x=4, y = -8/3 + 14/3 = 6/3 = 2 => (4,2). At x=3, y = -2 + 14/3 = ( -6 + 14)/3 = 8/3 ≈ 2.667, not integer. So only (1,4),(4,2). So that line covers two points.\n\nLine through (1,5) and (4,2): slope (5-2)/(1-4) = 3/ -3 = -1 (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,5) and (3,2): slope (5-2)/(1-3) = 3/ -2 = -1.5 (sunny). Equation: y - 5 = -1.5 (x - 1) => y = -1.5 x + 6.5. At x=3, y = -4.5 + 6.5 = 2 => (3,2). At x=4, y = -6 + 6.5 = 0.5 not integer. So line covers (1,5),(3,2). That's two points.\n\nThus we can cover (1,5) and (3,2) with a sunny line. Then we need to cover (1,4) and (4,2) maybe with another line, but we only have 4 lines total. Let's try to allocate:\n\nLine 1: y = x + 1 covering (1,2),(2,3).\nLine 2: y = x + 2 covering (1,3),(2,4).\nLine 3: y = -2x + 7 covering (1,5),(3,2). (We found earlier line y = -2x + 7 passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Actually (3,2) not on that line. Let's recompute: y = -2x + 7: at x=1, y=5; at x=3, y=1. So (3,2) not on that line. So we need line covering (1,5) and (3,2) we found earlier: slope -1.5 line: y = -1.5 x + 6.5 passes through (1,5),(3,2). That's a sunny line.\n\nLine 4: we need to cover (1,4) and (4,2) maybe with line slope -2/3 as above: y = -2/3 x + 14/3 passes through (1,4),(4,2). That's a sunny line.\n\nNow check coverage:\n\n- Non-sunny y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1).\n- L1: (1,2),(2,3).\n- L2: (1,3),(2,4).\n- L3: (1,5),(3,2).\n- L4: (1,4),(4,2).\n\nNow we need to cover (2,2),(3,3) and maybe (2,2) and (3,3) are not covered. Also (2,2) and (3,3) are not covered. We have used all 4 sunny lines. So we need to adjust to include (2,2) and (3,3). Perhaps we can replace one of the lines with a line covering (2,2),(3,3) and something else.\n\nWe can replace L1 (1,2),(2,3) with line y = x covering (2,2),(3,3). But then (1,2) and (2,3) become uncovered. However (1,2) could be covered by line L4? No. (2,3) could be covered by line L2? L2 currently covers (1,3),(2,4). Not (2,3). Could we adjust L2 to cover (2,3) as well? L2 is y = x + 2 covering (1,3),(2,4). If we change L2 to y = x + 1 covering (1,2),(2,3) we already used that. So we need to find a line covering (2,3) and maybe (1,4) or (3,2) etc.\n\nLet's try a different set of lines:\n\nGoal: cover (2,2),(3,3) with a sunny line: y = x (line L_a). That covers (2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(4,2). That's 8 points left, to be covered by 3 sunny lines (since we have used one sunny line L_a). Each line can cover up to 3 points, total capacity 9 >8, so possible.\n\nNow we need to find 3 lines covering these 8 points.\n\nWe can try to use line L_b: y = -2x + 7 covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). (3,1) covered by y=1. So L_b covers (1,5),(2,3). Good.\n\nNow remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,4),(3,2),(4,2). That's 6 points, to be covered by 2 sunny lines. Each line can cover up to 3 points, so we need each to be a triple.\n\nFind a line covering (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) not allowed. So maybe (1,2) and (3,2) horizontal not allowed. (1,2) and (4,2) horizontal not allowed. (1,2) and (2,4) slope 2, line y = 2x? Actually line through (1,2) and (2,4) is y = 2x, which also passes through (3,6) not in S_5. So only two points.\n\nFind a line covering (1,3),(2,4),(3,5) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFind a line covering (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFind a line covering (3,2),(4,2) horizontal not allowed.\n\nFind a line covering (3,2),(2,4) slope -2, line y = -2x + 8 passes through (3,2),(2,4). That's two points.\n\nFind a line covering (4,2),(2,4) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nFind a line covering (1,3),(3,2) slope -0.5, line y = -0.5 x + 3.5 passes through (1,3),(3,2). That's two points.\n\nFind a line covering (1,4),(4,2) slope -2/3, line y = -2/3 x + 14/3 passes through (1,4),(4,2). That's two points.\n\nThus we cannot find triples among these 6 points. So we need to use overlapping lines to cover them with 2 lines, each covering at most 3 points, but overlapping may allow covering all 6 points with 2 lines if each line covers 3 distinct points, but we need triples. However we cannot find triples, so we cannot cover all 6 points with 2 lines. So k=4 for n=5 seems impossible with y=1 as non-sunny line.\n\nThus maybe the answer set is wrong for n=5, k=4. The solution claims k=4 is possible for n=5, but we haven't found a construction. Let's try to see if any known results exist. The problem is from some contest; maybe the answer set is indeed {0,1,...,n-1} \\ {n/2 if n even} plus k=n for n=3. That includes k=4 for n=5. So we need to verify if k=4 is indeed possible for n=5. Let's try to find a construction for n=5,k=4 using a different non-sunny line, maybe diagonal x+y=6 (non-sunny). That covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points. Then we need to cover the remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 10 points, to be covered by 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can have at most 3 points, total capacity 12 >10, so possible.\n\nNow we need to cover these 10 points with 4 sunny lines. Let's try to find triples.\n\nPoints left: column x=1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Row y=1: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Also points (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). So set: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 10 points.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines covering them.\n\nWe can try to use line y = x covering (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) but (3,3) is covered by diagonal line, but we can still use it. So L1: y = x covers (1,1),(2,2). Good.\n\nWe can use line y = 2x covering (1,2),(2,4) but (2,4) is covered by diagonal. So L2: y = 2x covers (1,2). Actually (2,4) is covered, but we can still use L2 to cover (1,2). It also passes through (3,6) not in S_5. So L2 covers only (1,2) new.\n\nWe can use line y = -x + 5 (non-sunny) not allowed.\n\nWe can use line y = -2x + 5 passes through (1,3),(2,1). That's sunny (slope -2). So L3: y = -2x + 5 covers (1,3),(2,1). Good.\n\nWe can use line y = -0.5 x + 3.5 passes through (1,3),(3,2). But (1,3) already covered by L3. So L4: y = -0.5 x + 3.5 covers (3,2) new, (1,3) already covered.\n\nNow we have used 4 sunny lines: L1 (y=x) covering (1,1),(2,2); L2 (y=2x) covering (1,2); L3 (y=-2x+5) covering (1,3),(2,1); L4 (y=-0.5x+3.5) covering (3,2). Let's see coverage:\n\n- Diagonal x+y=6 covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\n- L1: (1,1),(2,2)\n- L2: (1,2)\n- L3: (1,3),(2,1)\n- L4: (3,2)\n\nNow uncovered points: (1,4),(2,3),(3,1),(4,1). That's 4 points left, but we have used all 4 sunny lines. So fails.\n\nWe need to adjust lines to cover more points.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = -x + 5 (non-sunny) not allowed. So we need to find sunny lines that cover more points.\n\nLet's try to use line y = -x + 4 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = -x + 3 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nThus we need to find sunny lines that cover points like (1,4) and (2,3) together. Let's see slope between (1,4) and (2,3): (4-3)/(1-2) = 1/ -1 = -1 (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\n(1,4) and (3,2): slope (4-2)/(1-3) = 2/ -2 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\n(1,4) and (4,1): slope (4-1)/(1-4) = 3/ -3 = -1 (non-sunny). So (1,4) cannot be paired with any other point except maybe (2,2) slope (4-2)/(1-2) = 2/ -1 = -2 (sunny). So line y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0). So L5: (1,4),(2,2). (2,2) already covered by L1. So L5 covers (1,4) new.\n\nNow (2,3) can be paired with (1,2) slope (3-2)/(2-1) = 1/1 = 1 (sunny). So line y = x + 1 passes through (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) not in S_5. So L6: (1,2),(2,3). (1,2) already covered by L2. So L6 covers (2,3) new.\n\nNow (3,1) can be paired with (2,2) slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed. Pair (3,1) with (1,2) slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5 (sunny). So line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope -0.5: equation y = -0.5 x + c => at (1,2): 2 = -0.5 + c => c = 2.5. So line y = -0.5 x + 2.5 passes through (3,1): y = -1