 lines (4)\u2013(5).  \n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover the whole triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   **Distinctness.**  \n   The lines in (4) have distinct intercepts \\(k+1+r\\); the lines in (5) have distinct intercepts \\(r\\).  \n   No line from (4) can coincide with a line from (5) because their slopes differ.  \n   Hence we indeed have \\(k\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Complete description of admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\)* \u2013 realised by the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)* \u2013 the construction of \u00a74 works (the vertical lines supply the non\u2011sunny part, the families (4)\u2013(5) supply the \\(k\\) sunny lines).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\)* \u2013 possible only when \\(n=3\\); the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover the three points of \\(S_3\\). For \\(n\\ge4\\) the counting argument (3) shows impossibility.  \n\n   Consequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;\n          \\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n   \\]"
