false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;\n        \\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound for a sunny line inside a right‑angled triangle.**  \n   In the triangle \\(T_k=\\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\}\\) each sunny line meets at most one point of every anti‑diagonal\n   \\(\\{x+y=t\\}\\;(2\\le t\\le k+1)\\); consequently a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s(k)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility for even \\(k\\) (and for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)).**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered form the triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\mid 1\\le x\\le k,\\;1\\le y\\le k+1-x\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and must cover \\(T_k\\).  \n   By (1) each of them can cover at most \\(s(k)\\) points, hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s(k)\\ge |T_k|\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (3) holds **only when \\(k\\) is odd**; for even \\(k\\) the left–hand side equals\n   \\(\\dfrac{k^{2}}2<\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   Therefore no configuration exists for even \\(k\\) (including the case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)).\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  \n   After the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) have been placed, we have to cover the triangle \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Define the following \\(k\\) lines (all slopes are \\(-2\\) or \\(1\\), hence sunny).\n\n   *Family A (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   A_r:\\;y=-2x+(k+1+r)\\qquad (r=0,1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Family B (slope \\(1\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   B_r:\\;y=x+r\\qquad (r=0,1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k=2m+1\\) lines are \\(\\{A_0,\\dots ,A_m,B_0,\\dots ,B_{m-1}\\}\\) together with the line \\(B_m\\); i.e. all lines listed in (4) and (5).\n\n   **Verification that the \\(k\\) lines cover \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) and put \\(t=x+y\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\)).  \n   Two cases.\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(t\\) is even.*  \n   Write \\(t=2s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le m+1\\).  \n   Choose the line \\(A_{s-1}\\) from (4).  \n   For this line we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y=-2x+(k+1+s-1)= -2x+(k+s).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(y=t-x=2s-x\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2s-x=-2x+k+s\\Longrightarrow x=k-s .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le s\\le m+1\\) and \\(k=2m+1\\), the value \\(x=k-s\\) satisfies \\(1\\le x\\le k\\).  \n   Consequently \\(y=t-x=2s-(k-s)=k+2s-2s=k\\ge1\\) and the point \\((x,y)\\) indeed lies on \\(A_{s-1}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(t\\) is odd.*  \n   Write \\(t=2s+1\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le m\\).  \n   Choose the line \\(B_{s}\\) from (5).  \n   For this line we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x+s .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(y=t-x=2s+1-x\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2s+1-x = x+s\\Longrightarrow x=s+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(0\\le s\\le m\\), we have \\(1\\le x\\le m+1\\le k\\); then  \n\n   \\[\n   y=t-x=2s+1-(s+1)=s .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(s\\ge0\\) and \\(y\\ge1\\) in the triangle, the only possible values are \\(s\\ge1\\); when \\(s=0\\) the point is \\((1,1)\\), which belongs to the line \\(B_0\\) as well (because \\(y=x\\)).  \n   Hence every point with odd \\(t\\) lies on the appropriate line \\(B_s\\).\n\n   In both cases the point \\((x,y)\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines (4)–(5).  \n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover the whole triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   **Distinctness.**  \n   The lines in (4) have distinct intercepts \\(k+1+r\\); the lines in (5) have distinct intercepts \\(r\\).  \n   No line from (4) can coincide with a line from (5) because their slopes differ.  \n   Hence we indeed have \\(k\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Complete description of admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\)* – realised by the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)* – the construction of §4 works (the vertical lines supply the non‑sunny part, the families (4)–(5) supply the \\(k\\) sunny lines).  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\)* – possible only when \\(n=3\\); the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover the three points of \\(S_3\\). For \\(n\\ge4\\) the counting argument (3) shows impossibility.  \n\n   Consequently the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;\n          \\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathemati