t‑angled lattice triangle of size \\(k\\) and contains\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nAll remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and must cover \\(T_k\\).\nBy (2) each of them can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(T_k\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\) and the\nleft–hand side of (3) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\), which is **strictly smaller**\nthan the right–hand side \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  Hence (3) cannot hold.\nThus **no even value of \\(k\\) is possible** (in particular \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible for \\(n\\ge4\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then each of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals\n\\(\\{a+b=t\\}\\;(2\\le t\\le n+1)\\) would have to be met by at least one line.\nA sunny line meets a given anti‑diagonal in **at most one** point\n(because two points on the same anti‑diagonal would have \\(\\Delta x+\\Delta y=0\\),\ncontradicting (1)).  Hence the total number of incidences between the\n\\(n\\) lines and the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(S_n\\) is at most \\(n\\cdot n\\).\nSince \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\), some point would remain uncovered.\nTherefore **\\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and clearly\ncover every point of \\(S_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Existence for every odd \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n-1)\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be odd and write \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).\nAgain we first use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); the\nremaining points are the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\nWe now exhibit **\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\) exactly**.\nFor \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,m\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{j}:\\;y = x + j ,\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} ,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand for \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{m+1+j}:\\;x+2y = k+2+j ,\\qquad\\text{(slope }-\\tfrac12\\text{)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines in (4) and (5) are sunny, they are pairwise distinct,\nand there are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(m+1)+(m)=2m+1=k\n\\]\n\nof them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Points covered by the lines of type (4)\n\nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(\\ell_{j}\\) inside \\(T_k\\) are those\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=a+j\\), \\(a\\ge1\\), \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-j}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k=2m+1\\) and \\(0\\le j\\le m\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-j}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2m+2-j}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =m+1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{j}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell_{j}\\) contains exactly the number of points shown in (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.2  Points covered by the lines of type (5)\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{m+1+j}\\) iff \\(a+2b=k+2+j\\).\nTogether with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) this gives  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{k+1-a}{1},\\qquad\nb=\\frac{k+2+j-a}{2}.\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(a\\) we obtain the admissible values of \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{j+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le b\\le m+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of points on \\(\\ell_{m+1+j}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm+1-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{j+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1\n   =m+1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{j}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.3  No overlap and complete coverage  \n\nFrom (6) and (7) we see that for each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(0\\le t\\le m\\) the two lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\) and \\(\\ell_{m+1+t}\\) each contain\n\\(m+1-\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor\\) points, and the sets of points they contain are\ndisjoint (the equations \\(y=x+t\\) and \\(x+2y=k+2+t\\) cannot be satisfied\nsimultaneously for positive integers \\(a,b\\)).  \n\nSumming the numbers of points contributed by all \\(k\\) lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^{m}\\bigl(m+1-\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   +\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}\\bigl(m+1-\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k| .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(k\\) lines listed in (4)–(5) cover **every** point of \\(T_k\\)\nexactly once.  Adding the previously placed \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the two conditions of the\nproblem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 7.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points\n\\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nAll four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are realizable, e.g.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2x-1\\);\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=-2x+5\\);\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=-2x+5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\n* No even value of \\(k\\) is possible (Section 3).  \n* For \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Section 4).  \n* \\(k=0\\) is always possible (Section 5).  \n* For every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists\n  (Section 6).  \n* When \\(n=3\\) the additional value \\(k=3\\) is also attainable\n  (Section 7).\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated\nin the boxed answer at the beginning of this solution. ∎"
