.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y = i\\,x + (k+2-i).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is **sunny**\n   (it is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and not \\(-1\\)).  The \\(k\\) lines\n   \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Verification that the sunny lines cover \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\).*  \n   Let \\((x',y')\\in\\Delta_{k+1}\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=k+2-y'-x'.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x',y'\\ge1\\) and \\(x'+y'\\le k+1\\), we have\n   \\[\n   1\\le i\\le k .\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(i\\) from (6) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = i\\,x + (k+2-i)\n     = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + (k+2-(k+2-y'-x'))\n     = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + y'+x' .\n   \\]\n\n   Taking \\(x=x'\\) we obtain \\(y=y'\\); hence \\((x',y')\\) satisfies the\n   equation of \\(S_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) lies\n   on one of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\).\n\n   *Putting everything together.*  \n   The complete family of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k-1},D\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   It consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n   \\(k\\) are sunny.  The non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except\n   those of \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\); the sunny lines cover the latter, so every\n   point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with at most one non‑sunny line exists.\n   The anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)) can contain at most one\n   point from each sunny line, so the single non‑sunny line must contain\n   the remaining point; consequently it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n   The next anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) (size \\(n-1\\)) meets that non‑sunny line\n   in no point, while each sunny line contributes at most one point.\n   Hence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_n\\) can be covered, but we need\n   \\(n-1\\) distinct points – they would have to be taken by the sunny\n   lines, leaving no sunny line free to meet the following anti‑diagonal.\n   Continuing this argument shows that some point of \\(T_n\\) stays uncovered,\n   a contradiction.  Therefore configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or\n   \\(k=n\\) do **not** exist.\n\nFrom the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for every\n\\(k\\le n-2\\) we obtain the complete description of the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one is non‑sunny; i.e. the\nnumber of sunny lines is \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\nConsider the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points.\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\); therefore it meets\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) in **at most one** point.\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n\\(D_{n+1}\\); the remaining point must lie on the unique non‑sunny line.\nHence that line is parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nNow look at the next anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has \\(n-1\\) points.\nThe non‑sunny line, being parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\), meets \\(D_{n}\\) in **no**\npoint.  Each sunny line can again meet \\(D_{n}\\) in at most one point,\nso at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\) can be covered.\nBecause \\(k\\ge n-1\\), we would need **all** \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\)\nto be taken by the sunny lines, leaving none of them available for the\nnext anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\).  But \\(D_{n-1}\\) also contains points,\nand neither the sunny lines (already used) nor the non‑sunny line\n(parallel to \\(D_{n+1}\\)) can meet it.  Hence some point of \\(T_n\\) stays\nuncovered – a contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) cannot exist; we have\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**(a) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\[2mm]\nD   &: x+y=n-k+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\nand \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); all are non‑sunny.\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except the set\n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k\\text{ and }a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**(b) Shape of the uncovered set.**  \nWrite \\(a=n-k-1+u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\).  Condition (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nu\\ge1,\\qquad u+b\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence, after the affine change of variables  \n\n\\[\n(x',y')=(u,b),\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(U\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{k+1}:=\\{(x',y')\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid x',y'\\ge1,\\;x'+y'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) is a right‑angled triangular lattice containing\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n---\n\n**(c) Sunny lines covering the triangle.**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (k+2-i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny\n(\\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((x',y')\\in\\Delta_{k+1}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=k+2-y'-x'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x',y'\\ge1\\) and \\(x'+y'\\le k+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\nSubstituting \\(i\\) from (6) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = i\\,x + (k+2-i)\n  = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + (k+2-(k+2-y'-x'))\n  = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + y'+x' .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(x=x'\\) yields \\(y=y'\\); thus \\((x',y')\\) satisfies the\nequation of \\(S_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) lies on one\nof the sunny lines \\(S_i\\).\n\n---\n\n**(d) The whole configuration.**  \nCollect all lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k-1},D\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThere are \\((n-k-1)+1=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines,\naltogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except those of\n\\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\); the sunny lines cover exactly \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) by the\nverification above.  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least\none line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) (the case \\(k=n\\) is even stronger).\nThen exactly one line, say \\(M\\), is non‑sunny.\n\nAs in the proof of the upper bound, the anti‑diagonal\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)) can contain at most one point from each sunny\nline, so \\(M\\) must contain the remaining point; consequently \\(M\\) is\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nThe next anti‑diagonal \\(D_n\\) (size \\(n-1\\)) meets \\(M\\) in no point.\nEach of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most one point,\nhence they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_n\\).  To cover all\n\\(n-1\\) points, every sunny line would have to be used on \\(D_n\\), leaving\nnone of them available for the following anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\).\nBut \\(D_{n-1}\\) also contains points, and neither \\(M\\) (parallel to\n\\(D_{n+1}\\)) nor any sunny line (already exhausted) can meet it.\nThus some point of \\(T_n\\) stays uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nTherefore configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) do not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for\nevery \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) together give the complete set of\nadmissible values:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
