irwise distinct.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((x',y')\\in\\Delta_{k+1}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=k+2-y'-x'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x',y'\\ge1\\) and \\(x'+y'\\le k+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\nSubstituting \\(i\\) from (6) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = i\\,x + (k+2-i)\n  = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + (k+2-(k+2-y'-x'))\n  = (k+2-y'-x')\\,x + y'+x' .\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(x=x'\\) yields \\(y=y'\\); thus \\((x',y')\\) satisfies the\nequation of \\(S_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) lies on one\nof the sunny lines \\(S_i\\).\n\n---\n\n**(d) The whole configuration.**  \nCollect all lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k-1},D\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThere are \\((n-k-1)+1=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines,\naltogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except those of\n\\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\); the sunny lines cover exactly \\(\\Delta_{k+1}\\) by the\nverification above.  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least\none line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) (the case \\(k=n\\) is even stronger).\nThen exactly one line, say \\(M\\), is non\u2011sunny.\n\nAs in the proof of the upper bound, the anti\u2011diagonal\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)) can contain at most one point from each sunny\nline, so \\(M\\) must contain the remaining point; consequently \\(M\\) is\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nThe next anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_n\\) (size \\(n-1\\)) meets \\(M\\) in no point.\nEach of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most one point,\nhence they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_n\\).  To cover all\n\\(n-1\\) points, every sunny line would have to be used on \\(D_n\\), leaving\nnone of them available for the following anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{n-1}\\).\nBut \\(D_{n-1}\\) also contains points, and neither \\(M\\) (parallel to\n\\(D_{n+1}\\)) nor any sunny line (already exhausted) can meet it.\nThus some point of \\(T_n\\) stays uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) do not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for\nevery \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) together give the complete set of\nadmissible values:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]"

