d a covering with all sunny lines for n=4 and we didn't find one. Let's test if k=4 is possible.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines covering all points. Since we have no non-sunny lines, each line can intersect column 1 at most once. So we need 4 lines to cover the 4 points in column 1. So each line must intersect column 1 at a distinct point (1,b). So we have lines L_b passing through (1,b) for b=1..4. Each line L_b has slope m_b (non-zero, not -1). At column a, the point is (a, b + (a-1) m_b). We need to cover all points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4?). Actually column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). So we need to cover these three points with the four lines. Since we have four lines, we can assign each of the three points to three of the lines, and the fourth line may produce a point outside the region.\n\nThus we need to find slopes m_b such that for each b, the point at column 2 is (2, something) = b + m_b. So we need the set {b + m_b | b=1..4} to contain {1,2,3}. Since we have four values, we can have one extra value outside the set.\n\nThus we need to assign m_b such that b + m_b = some integer between 1 and 3 for three of the b's, and the fourth b + m_b can be anything else.\n\nSimilarly, for column 3: points (3,1),(3,2). We need the set {b + 2 m_b} to contain {1,2}.\n\nFor column 4: point (4,1). Need {b + 3 m_b} to contain 1.\n\nWe need to find m_b rational numbers satisfying these constraints.\n\nLet's try to find a solution.\n\nLet’s denote unknown slopes m1,m2,m3,m4.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- For column 2: values v_i = b_i + m_i (where b_i = i). Need {v_i} to contain {1,2,3}. So we need three of the v_i equal 1,2,3 (in some order). The fourth v_i can be any integer (maybe >3 or ≤0). Also v_i must be integer because we need integer points. So m_i must be integer (since b_i integer). So m_i must be integer.\n\nThus m_i must be integer for each i (since v_i integer). So slopes must be integer.\n\nNow for column 3: w_i = b_i + 2 m_i must contain {1,2}. So for each i,