agonals.\n\nWe need to find all possible k such that there exists a configuration of n lines covering all points.\n\nWe need to consider constraints: There are n columns (x=1,...,n) that intersect the triangle? Actually the points have a from 1 to n (since a+b ≤ n+1 and b≥1, a can be up to n). Similarly b from 1 to n. So there are n possible x-values (columns) and n possible y-values (rows). Also there are n possible anti-diagonals x+y = c for c = 2,...,n+1 (since a+b≥2). So there are n anti-diagonals.\n\nA non-sunny line can cover all points in a column (vertical line), all points in a row (horizontal line), or all points in an anti-diagonal (slope -1 line). So each non-sunny line can cover a whole family of points.\n\nWe have n lines total. If we use only non-sunny lines, we could cover all points using n lines: e.g., use all n vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. That covers all points. So k=0 is possible for any n≥3. Similarly, we can use n horizontal lines y=1,...,y=n. So k=0 is possible.\n\nNow, can we have k=1? Use n-1 non-sunny lines and 1 sunny line. The solution suggests using n-1 vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1, covering all points except those with x=n. The only uncovered point is (n,1). Actually points with x=n and a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ 1. So only (n,1) is uncovered. So we can add a sunny line through (n,1). That works. So k=1 is possible.\n\nNow, can we have k=2? The solution claims impossible for n≥4. Let's examine if we can find a configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines and n total lines covering all points. The solution uses a pigeonhole argument: With n-2 non-sunny lines, they can cover at most n-2 families among columns, rows, anti-diagonals. Since there are n families of each type, at least one column, one row, and one anti-diagonal are uncovered. Then they pick smallest uncovered column a and smallest uncovered row b, and note that anti-diagonal x+y=a+b is also uncovered. Then points (a,b), (a+1,b), (a,b+1) are all in T_n and not on any non-sunny line. Since th