ld try to reason.\n\nWe have 10 points. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point. So each column can have at most 4 points covered (since we have 4 lines). Column 1 has 4 points, so each line must intersect column 1 at a distinct point. So each line must pass through exactly one of (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So we have 4 lines, each assigned to one of those points. Similarly, each row can have at most 4 points covered. Row 1 has points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). So each line must intersect row 1 at a distinct point. So each line must also pass through exactly one of those points. So we need a bijection between the set of lines and the points in column 1 and row 1. That is, each line must pass through a unique point in column 1 and a unique point in row 1. So we need a permutation mapping column points to row points: each line passes through (1,b_i) and (a_i,1) for some b_i ∈ {1,2,3,4} and a_i ∈ {1,2,3,4}, with each b_i distinct and each a_i distinct. So we need a bijection between {1,2,3,4} and itself: a_i = σ(b_i) for some permutation σ. So each line passes through (1,b) and (σ(b),1). The line connecting these two points has slope (1 - b) / (σ(b) - 1). This slope must not be 0, ∞, -1. Let's compute slope: m = (1 - b) / (σ(b) - 1). For b=1, (1-1)=0 => slope 0, which is not allowed (horizontal). So we cannot have a line passing through (1,1) and any (a,1) because that would be horizontal (slope 0). So we cannot assign b=1 to any line that also passes through a point in row 1 (a,1) with a ≠ 1. Actually (1,1) is intersection of column 1 and row 1. So one line could be assigned to (1,1) for both column and row, i.e., the line passes through (1,1) only, not any other point in column 1 or row 1. But each line must intersect column 1 at exactly one point, and row 1 at exactly one point. If a line passes through (1,1), then it intersects both column 1 and row 1 at the same point. That would mean that column 1 and row 1 each have one point covered by that line