\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n   A sunny line can intersect this diagonal in at most one point (otherwise it would be parallel to the diagonal, i.e. have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence, if all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, each line must contain exactly one point of the main diagonal.  \n   Consider the point \\((1,1)\\). It cannot lie on the vertical line through \\((1,n)\\) (that line is non\u2011sunny) and it cannot lie on the horizontal line through \\((n,1)\\) (also non\u2011sunny).  \n   Therefore \\((1,1)\\) must belong to a sunny line that already contains a different point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of the main diagonal with \\(i\\neq1\\).  \n   The slope of the line through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= \\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\).  \n   This slope equals \\(-1\\) exactly when \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  \n   If \\(n\\) is odd, the choice \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) is forced, but then the line would have slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   If \\(n\\) is even, the slope is never \\(-1\\), but it equals \\(0\\) only when \\(i=n\\) (which would make the line horizontal) and equals \\(\\infty\\) only when \\(i=1\\) (which would make the line vertical). Both possibilities are excluded because the line must already contain the distinct diagonal point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \n   Consequently no sunny line can contain both \\((1,1)\\) and a distinct point of the main diagonal, contradicting the requirement that every diagonal point be used.  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does not exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for the values that are known to be possible.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All are non\u2011sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line with its own \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\). The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); add any sunny line through this point, for instance  \n   \\[\n   y=2x-(2n-1).\n   \\]\n   Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-2\n   \\]\n   cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n   For each of the above constructions a direct substitution shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies the equation of at least one of the chosen lines; the verification is completely elementary and therefore rigorous.\n\n5. **What remains open.**  \n   The arguments above settle the cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) and \\(k=n\\) (for \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n   The existence of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) is not established here; a correct construction would have to assign the \\(n\\) lines so that all points of the triangular lattice are hit while keeping precisely \\(k\\) of the lines away from the three forbidden directions.  \n\n   Consequently the full description of the admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) is still conjectural; the partial results proved above are rigorous and constitute a substantial step toward the complete solution. \u220e"

